<ShortAnswer>
  Chrome's own sync needs a Google account. Without one, carry your tabs over as a bookmarks file,
  or use a tab manager with its own sync: in TabCabin you create an account with a username and
  password, or run the sync server yourself.
</ShortAnswer>

<h2>What Chrome needs a Google account for</h2>
<p>
  Bookmarks, history, saved tab groups and the tabs open on your other devices all travel through
  Chrome sync, and Chrome sync works only when you sign in to the browser with a Google account. On
  a work or school computer that is often switched off, and some people would rather keep their
  browsing out of their Google account.
</p>

<h2>1. Carry a bookmarks file</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    On the first computer, press <kbd>Ctrl+Shift+D</kbd> to bookmark all tabs of the window into a
    folder.
  </li>
  <li>
    Open the bookmark manager with <kbd>Ctrl+Shift+O</kbd>, then ⋮ → Export bookmarks, and save the
    file.
  </li>
  <li>
    On the other computer, open the bookmark manager, choose ⋮ → Import bookmarks and pick the file.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  It works in every browser, but it is a copy: what you change on one computer reaches the other
  only when you export and import again.
</p>

<h2>2. A TabCabin account with a username and password</h2>
<p>
  TabCabin is a free, open-source extension that keeps your tabs, links, notes and to-dos on boards
  in the new tab page. It works offline on its own, and syncs through the TabCabin sync server when
  you sign in.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Get TabCabin on both computers.</li>
  <li>
    On the first one, open Settings → Account & sync, choose “or with a username and password” and
    create an account.
  </li>
  <li>
    On the second one, sign in with the same username and password. If that browser already has
    boards, choose Keep both or Use the server's data.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  From then on a change on one computer shows up on the other within seconds while both are online.
  Signing in with Google is there too, for anyone who prefers it, but you never need it.
</p>

<h2>3. Run your own sync server</h2>
<p>
  If you want the data only on a machine you own, run the TabCabin server with Docker on a small VPS
  or a Raspberry Pi, and build the extension with your server's address. The{' '}
  <a href="/self-hosted-tab-manager/">self-hosted tab manager</a> page explains the whole setup.
</p>

